er candidate. We should consider the criteria.\n\nOur own notes: Candidate A: anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities, regarded as a show-off, not open to new ideas. No info on reliability, stress, crew cooperation.\n\nCandidate B: very conscientious, handles stress very well, good at assessing weather conditions, excellent computer skills, relatively weak memory for numbers, makes nasty remarks about colleagues. So concerns: professional communication, crew cooperation.\n\nCandidate C: can make correct decisions quickly, keeps calm in a crisis, understands complicated technology, difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not very willing to further his education. Also earlier note: reliability, positive crew atmosphere.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as a know-it-all, hot temper.\n\nWe need to discuss missing criteria: stress resilience, crew cooperation, professional communication, adaptability, decision quality, etc.\n\nAgent 1's argument: recommends C based on reliability, positive crew atmosphere, quick correct decisions, but acknowledges concerns.\n\nWe could ask for more info about Candidate B's crew cooperation (maybe via tool). Or we could bring up Candidate D's hot temper and know-it-all attitude as concerns. Or we could argue that Candidate B's stress handling and conscientiousness outweigh his communication issues, making him a better fit.\n\nWe need to decide our vote. We could either stay with Candidate C, or shift to B or D. Let's evaluate.\n\nKey criteria for long-distance pilot: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention, crew cooperation, professional communication, responsibility, adaptability.\n\nCandidate C: reliability (yes), crew atmosphere (yes), quick correct decisions (yes), calm in crisis (yes), understands complicated tech (yes). Concerns: difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education (adaptability). So crew cooperation may be okay (positive atmosphere) but communication difficulty may affect crew. Egocentric may affect teamwork.\n\nCandidate B: very conscientious (reliability), handles stress very well (stress resilience), good at assessing weather (technical competence), excellent computer skills (technical), but weak memory for numbers (attention), makes nasty remarks (professional communication, crew cooperation). So strong on reliability and stress, but poor on communication and teamwork.\n\nCandidate D: responsibility, problem solving, concentration, responds to unexpected events (stress), but hot temper (crew cooperation), know-it-all (communication). So mixed.\n\nCandidate A: leadership, anticipates danger, spatial vision, sees complex connections (technical), but show-off, not open to new ideas (communication, adaptability). No info on reliability or stress.\n\nThus the strongest overall may be C (reliability, crew atmosphere, decision quality) despite communication issues. B has stress but communication issues severe. D has temper.\n\nThus I might stay with C, but ask for more info on B's crew cooperation to compare. Or I could shift to B if I think stress handling is more critical. But the panel hasn't discussed stress for C beyond calm in crisis, which is good. So I think staying with C is reasonable.\n\nWe need to reference Agent 1's points and add our own evidence. Provide a concise argument.\n\nThus public message: I agree with Agent 1 that C's reliability and quick decision-making are strong, and his calmness in crisis supports stress resilience. However, I note his difficulty communicating ideas and egocentric attitude could affect crew cooperation, but his positive crew atmosphere suggests he mitigates that. Compared to B, whose stress handling is good but his nasty remarks raise serious crew cooper